<p><strong>The Stutsman-Wyatt Home</strong></p>
<p><strong>510 3rd St NW</strong></p>
<p>On the corner of this property, one of the only surveying cement pillars in Mandan is still standing, a remnant of Mandan’s platting of this area of the city in the early 1900s. {% person "w-h-stutsman" %}W.H. Stutsman{% endperson %}, son of a civil war surgeon, was an early resident of this home, and worked as the state railroad commissioner for 10 years. He became and remained an attorney for 47 years in Mandan. Arriving in Mandan in 1903 with his wife, {% person "ada-sharples-stutsman" %}Ada Sharples{% endperson %}, from his native state of Iowa, he was one of the founders of the {% place "independent-voters-association" %}Independent Voters Assn.{% endplace %} and a member of the {% place "north-dakota-workmens-compensation-bureau" %}Workmen’s Compensation Bureau{% endplace %} for three years. In 1950 he had the distinction of being the oldest Elk member in the state, at the age of 84, the same year he passed away.</p>
<p>After an unsuccessful attempt to purchase the former {% place "first-street-federal-building" %}Federal Building on 1st St NW{% endplace %}, the {% place "first-nazarene-church" %}First Nazarene Church{% endplace %}, {% person "doug-wyatt" %}Pastor Doug{% endperson %} and {% person "joni-wyatt" %}Joni Wyatt{% endperson %} along with their daughter {% person "mckenna-wyatt" %}McKenna{% endperson %}, and two sons, {% person "mac-wyatt" %}Mac{% endperson %}, and {% person "lane-wyatt" %}Lane{% endperson %}, moved into this home in 2004.</p>
<p>While Joni and her daughter were in the attic sweeping, they discovered a long forgotten item of W.H. Stutsman. Carefully unwrapping it, they discovered a hand written note on stationery of the Department of State, Bismarck, ND, which said “Mandan, ND November 11, 1918. I paid $1 for this flag (which goes to the YMCA drive which starts on this day) and marched with it in the Jubilation Parade at 2nd Ave. W.H. Stutsman”. Within this rolled-up letter was a flag with only 48 stars on it. Joni showed the flag to her children to see if they could tell how it was different from our current flag. They didn’t realize there had ever been a flag with 48 stars. The Wyatts are founding members of the Mandan Historical Society.</p>
